Just remember that early on your rogue isn't a wombat.
If I can, I avoid all melee combat until I get dagger
up to expert, and I'll only wield my short sword to
read-identify scrolls of enchant weapon. If you happen
to run across a hobbit with an elven dagger, snag it
and use it for your melee weapon to speed up your skill
advancement. 

Avoid using unproofed daggers on acid blobs - it makes
keeping a good stack together harder.

Making good use of doors and corridors will make dagger
tossing more effective - let them line up to die, because
a multi-dagger volley can kill the monster up front and
damage any behind it. A large stack of daggers will
allow you to keep moving out of melee range and still
manage a large crowd of npcs. Some will even be so kind
as to throw your daggers back to you.

Another good thing to remember is that just because a
monster is in melee range doesn't mean you have to stop
throwing things at him.

My typical skill progression for rogues is as follows:

1) daggers to expert
2) try to get an artifact, then
   a) broadsword to expert if I get SB (doesn't take long)
   b) dual-weapon with daggers to expert if I get any of 
      the other chaotic artifacts, using an elven dagger
	  as as secondary.
   c) if I'm unable to get an artifact, do b) with an
      elven dagger as a primary and (hopefully) secondary
3) do whatever I didn't get done in 2).
   - you'll probably have to gain more levels to complete
     this part, but once you've gotten the necessary hits
	 to advance, you can switch back to your best melee
	 weapon combination

By sticking with throwing daggers early you can avoid
taking damage. If you can, try not to take any melee
damage until the Gnomish Mines. Those three or four levels
will get you used to the to the types of combat movement
you'll be using for most of the rest of the game. Again,
you're not a wombat yet, so stick close to the stairs to
bolt and rest if you need.

Get and use a light source ASAP so you can get some ranged
attacks in to soften up your foes before you have to 
go face to face with them. Lit levels should be a walk
in the park - plenty of room to move around and not get
hit.

If you stick to a plan like this, you'll get your dagger
proficiency up to skilled around Mine Town.

